Translations README

Speedy MIDI uses the Qt translation system.

Use "lupdate speedymidi.pro" from "src/speedymidi" directory to generate or 
update the .ts files.

To edit the .ts files, use Qt Linguist.

Use "lrelease speedymidi.pro" from "src/speedymidi" directory to generate or 
update the .qm files. Then copy the .qm files to the directory of the executable,
subdirectory "translations".


FURTHER EXPLANATION

Speedy MIDI always uses 3 .qm files simultaneously to translate all its strings.
1) msg_XX.qm   from Speedy MIDI's src/translations
2) music_XX.qm from Speedy MIDI's src/translations
3) qt_XX.qm    from the Qt SDK, subdirectory qt/translations

where XX is replaced by the language identifier (en, de, ...)

The main program interface (about 90% of the strings) is translated by msg_XX.qm
All musical names and symbols (e.g. note names, key signatures, default track 
names etc.) is translated by music_XX.qm

The messages are partitioned into msg_XX.qm and music_XX.qm (so the 
translations are disjoint and complete).

The only string both .qm files must contain is in context <LanguageName> the 
translation for <Name> which gives the name of the language in the language's
format. This is used in the preferences dialog to show the available language 
names such as "English", "Deutsch", ...


SPECIAL CASES

msg_en.qm handles plural forms only, as all other strings in the source code 
are already in english.

qt_en.qm is a file providing no translations. It is a dummy file used to avoid 
warning messages when loading the translators in SpeedyMidiApp::setupTranslators().
